In article <1993Jan11.204430.12880@cs.cornell.edu>, bau@cs.cornell.edu (David Bau) writes:
>Every time I play, when I get a scroll of ID, I'm always in a conundrum
>when it comes to what order to ID things in.
>
>Which type of things are the best to ID first?  Which shouldn't you waste
>a scroll IDing?
>
>David

Normally amulets and rings first.  If you have a duplicate ring, try 
dropping one down a sink to identify it instead.

Identify scrolls only if you haven't discovered the nasty ones yet.
The nasty ones are: destroy armor, amnesia, and fire.  Otherwise,
if it's not cursed, just read it.

Potions I usually take a chance and drink (if they're not cursed),
and don't waste an identify scroll on.

Wands you should probably try to figure out by engraving first.  Using
identify to find the number of charges is a lower priority.

Weapons: If you have a good weapon (an artifact) you should identify it's
         enchantment, to know how far you can safely enchant it.  Aside
         from your main weapon, not much point identifying weapon.

Armor:   Only the strange boots, cloaks, helmets, and gloves you can't
         figure out.  To find the enchantment, try it on (unless it's 
         cursed -- I'd say don't bother keeping cursed armor).

Tools:   Most don't need to be identified.

Spellbooks: Don't identify them, just read them (unless they're cursed).

Food:    I think only tins and eggs can be identified.

Gems:    Only once you've done everything else.

So a typical order to use is "=?!/)](+%* 
Change it to suit your circurstances.


Of course, a better way to do that is to edit the source code and make
my modification to let you select items to be identified more easily :-)

